<Window xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:shell="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Shell;assembly=PresentationFramework"
        xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib"
        Title="Claude sessions"
        Width="1480" Height="980" MinWidth="620" MinHeight="420"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen"
        UseLayoutRounding="True"
        TextOptions.TextFormattingMode="Display"
        TextOptions.TextRenderingMode="Grayscale"
        Background="#08080A">

  <!-- 🔴 WHY THE TEXT LOOKED "PROGRAMMATIC".
       Three settings, and two of them were fighting the fonts:

         TextRenderingMode was ClearType. ClearType is SUBPIXEL antialiasing -
         it tints the edge of every stem red or blue to buy horizontal
         resolution. On white it disappears; on a #0F1013 ground it is visible
         colour fringing on every glyph, and that fringe is what reads as a
         utility program rather than a designed surface. Grayscale antialiasing
         is what modern dark UI uses, and what a phone renders.

         SnapsToDevicePixels forced glyph edges onto whole pixels, which is
         right for a 1px border and wrong for letterforms: it is what makes type
         look hand-drawn on a grid. UseLayoutRounding stays, so the hairlines
         are still crisp; the text is no longer nailed to the grid.

         TextFormattingMode is DISPLAY, and it used to be Ideal. The reasoning
         for Ideal was sound and is kept here because it is still true at large
         sizes: Display snaps advance widths to whole pixels and throws away the
         optical spacing a variable UI face exists to provide.

         🔴 AND IT DOES NOT SAY "Segoe UI Variable" ANY MORE, BECAUSE THE WINDOW
         HAS NEVER RUN ON IT. The three FontFamily entries below name Segoe, and
         Install-SRTypeface REPLACES all three with MANROPE at runtime before the
         window is shown - so every comment in this file that reasoned about
         Segoe's metrics was reasoning about a face that is not on screen. That
         is not a cosmetic slip: type decisions were made from it, including
         sizing the machine text to the terminal, and the two faces have
         different x-heights (Manrope 0.540 em, Segoe 0.500, Cascadia 0.518).
         Measure the face that is RUNNING.

         What that reasoning missed is the size this pane actually runs at. Ideal
         positions glyphs at SUB-PIXEL advances, so at 12px body text the gaps
         between characters land on different fractions of a pixel and the
         spacing reads as irregular - reported directly, in those words, after
         the prose was taken down to 12. Optical spacing is worth having on a
         28px display face; on 12px running text it buys nothing you can see and
         costs the evenness you can.

         🪤 This is a DIFFERENT setting from TextRenderingMode above, and only
         one of the two can be judged from a screenshot. Formatting mode changes
         advance widths, which a capture shows faithfully. Rendering mode is
         subpixel colour, and CONTEXT.md records that the test renderer always
         composites greyscale - so a captured 'cleartype' sample is greyscale
         wearing a label. That one has to be flipped and looked at.
       ================================================== -->

  <!-- ================== THE FRAME ==================
       NO OS TITLE BAR, but still a real window.

       🔴 WindowChrome, NOT WindowStyle=None. WindowStyle="None" alone throws
       away the frame AND everything the frame did: Aero Snap, edge resize,
       maximise-to-work-area, and the drop shadow. WindowChrome removes only the
       painted caption and lets Windows keep managing the rest, so dragging to
       an edge still snaps and the borders still resize.

       🔴 CaptionHeight IS THE HEADER PLUS THE INSET (46 + 14 = 60). The app is
       a card floating 14px inside the window, so the header no longer starts at
       the window's top edge - and the ground around the card should drag it too,
       which the same number gives for free.

       🔴 CaptionHeight MATCHES THE HEADER, and that is what makes this behave
       like a window. It was 0, which told Windows "there is no caption here" -
       so the only way to move the window was the app calling DragMove itself,
       and with it you lose everything Windows does with a caption: drag, snap to
       an edge, aero-shake, Win+arrow, double-click to maximise, and the system
       menu on right-click or Alt+Space. The window could be maximised and closed
       (those are our own buttons) but NOT MOVED, which is exactly what was
       reported.

       Every interactive control sitting in that strip opts back OUT with
       WindowChrome.IsHitTestVisibleInChrome="True" - without it Windows treats
       the click as a caption drag and the control never sees it. Anything on the
       strip WITHOUT that attribute is draggable surface, which is why the title
       text is not marked.

       GlassFrameThickness="0" keeps the DWM glass out of a dark window;
       UseAeroCaptionButtons="False" stops Windows drawing its own.
       ============================================== -->
  <shell:WindowChrome.WindowChrome>
    <shell:WindowChrome CaptionHeight="60"
                        ResizeBorderThickness="6"
                        CornerRadius="0"
                        GlassFrameThickness="0"
                        UseAeroCaptionButtons="False"/>
  </shell:WindowChrome.WindowChrome>

  <Window.Resources>

    <!-- ================== PALETTE ==================
         Colour is allowed here for the first time, and it carries exactly one
         meaning: WHAT A CONVERSATION NEEDS FROM YOU. Every accent is twinned
         with a glyph and a band heading, so the screen still reads correctly in
         a screenshot, on a monochrome display, or to somebody who cannot tell
         these hues apart. Colour is the fast path; it is never the only path.
         ============================================ -->
    <!-- 🔴 THERE IS NO HUE IN THIS WINDOW, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE IDEA.
         Colour used to mean four things at once - needs you, focus, primary,
         danger - so it meant none of them. White is now the only emphasis there
         is, and it is spent in exactly two ways, which do not compete because
         they are different KINDS of thing:
           as a STATE   white type and a lit edge, and only a conversation
                        waiting on you earns it
           as a SURFACE a filled white control - the one action a panel is for
         Everything else is a step down the grey ladder. That is why a single lit
         card reads across the room.

         The ladder is the LUMINANCE ladder: on a near-black ground, shadow does
         nothing and only lightness separates one surface from another. The steps
         are ~8 points, where the old palette's four surfaces sat within six
         points of EACH OTHER and read as one flat plane. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Ink"       Color="#08080A"/>   <!-- L0 the ground, around the window -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Panel"     Color="#0F0F11"/>   <!-- L1 the window itself -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="PanelHi"   Color="#16161A"/>   <!-- L2 a raised surface -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Rail"      Color="#0D0D0F"/>   <!-- L1- the sunken rail -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Sheet"     Color="#1B1B20"/>   <!-- L3 sheets and dialogs -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Hairline"  Color="#24242A"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HairSoft"  Color="#191920"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextMax"   Color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextHigh"  Color="#E4E6EB"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextMid"   Color="#9A9DA6"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextLow"   Color="#6C6F78"/>

    <!-- The bands. They are LUMINANCE STEPS, not colours: needs-you is pure
         white and everything else recedes from it. The band heading, the row's
         edge bar and the unread dot all take these, so a band still reads
         without colour vision, in a screenshot, and in greyscale print. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccNeeds"   Color="#FFFFFF"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccWorking" Color="#9EA2AC"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccDone"    Color="#71757E"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccIdle"    Color="#54575E"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="AccQuiet"   Color="#3A3D43"/>
    <!-- Focus is a neutral ring. It used to be the same clay as needs-you, so
         the keyboard's position and a demand for your attention were the same
         signal. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="Focus"      Color="#8E939D"/>

    <!-- ================== THE HUES ==================
         The monochrome rule above is AMENDED, not abandoned. Hue now carries
         exactly two things - WHO SPOKE and WHICH PROJECT - and nothing else.
         State stays a luminance step, so a band still reads in greyscale, in a
         screenshot and without colour vision; losing hue costs you the speaker
         label's colour and nothing that decides what needs attention.

         🪤 These do NOT compete with the band accents, because they never
         appear in the same place: bands live in the rail and the list, hues
         live inside the reading pane and on the project tiles.
         ============================================== -->
    <!-- 🔴 ONE RULE PER HUE, AND THE OLD SET HAD NONE. Reported as "I do not
         like the colouring, it feels like a random colouring" - and the reason
         it feels that way is structural rather than a matter of taste. Seven
         saturated hues at the same chroma were carrying THREE UNRELATED
         QUESTIONS in one channel: who is speaking (blue/orange), what kind of
         machinery ran (violet/green/teal), and how it went (green/amber/red).
         No rule can be inferred from a channel that answers three questions,
         so the eye reads it as noise.

         🪤 TWO PAIRS WERE ALSO INDISTINGUISHABLE. #FFB86B "you" against
         #FFD166 "a message from another session" is about 25 degrees of hue;
         #5EEAD4 "a question waiting on you" against #5FD68A "a background
         shell" is about the same. Two of those four meanings are the ones you
         most need to catch at a glance.

         Now each hue answers exactly one question, and the set is warmed to
         Claude's own clay rather than the neon pastels:

           HueOut  #D97757  YOU. Claude clay. Your turns also carry a ground.
           HueIn   #8FA8C8  ANOTHER SESSION speaking. Claude itself is PLAIN -
                            the reply is the default voice and needs no hue.
           HueTool #8A8578  THE MACHINE, all of it - runs, shells, sub-agents,
                            hooks, notices, file lists, thinking. The GLYPH
                            says which; the hue only says "not a person".
           HueAsk  #5EC8B7  SOMETHING IS WAITING ON YOU. The one bright accent
                            in the window, spent on nothing else.
           HueBad  #E2687E  IT FAILED. Pushed to magenta so it cannot be read
                            as the clay of your own turns.
           HueWarn #D9B45F  a caution in the chrome.
           HueOk   #7FB98A  a healthy state in the chrome.

         🪤 THE COST, STATED: a run of Reads and a run that spawned a sub-agent
         are now the same colour. That is deliberate - the marker table already
         gives them different SHAPES (filled square against an at-sign) and the
         note there records that shape, not hue, was always meant to carry the
         difference. Hue reinforced it; now it does not. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueIn"    Color="#8FA8C8"/>   <!-- came in  - claude speaking -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueOut"   Color="#D97757"/>   <!-- went out - you speaking -->
    <!-- 🔴 DIMMER THAN TextMid, ON PURPOSE, NOW THAT SHAPE CARRIES NOTHING.
         With one glyph for every kind, the ONLY thing separating Claude
         speaking from the machine working is this value against TextMid
         #9A9DA6 - and #8A8578 was within a few points of it, so the two read
         as one grey. The machine is meant to be quieter than the reply, not
         beside it. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueTool"  Color="#6F6A61"/>   <!-- the machinery between them -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueBad"   Color="#E2687E"/>   <!-- a call that failed -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueAsk"   Color="#5EC8B7"/>   <!-- a question waiting on you -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueWarn"  Color="#D9B45F"/>
    <!-- A context window with room left. Its own green rather than the teal
         HueAsk, which already means "a question is waiting on you" - a bar that
         borrowed it would have said that on every healthy session. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueOk"    Color="#7FB98A"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueOutWash"  Color="#D97757" Opacity="0.13"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueToolWash" Color="#6F6A61" Opacity="0.15"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CtxTrack"    Color="#000000" Opacity="0.38"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueAskWash"  Color="#5EC8B7" Opacity="0.10"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueAskFilm"  Color="#5EC8B7" Opacity="0.05"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HueAskEdge"  Color="#5EC8B7" Opacity="0.34"/>

    <!-- The bevel: a dark outer stroke with a light inner highlight, so a
         surface looks lit from above rather than outlined. One pair, used by
         every raised thing in the window. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BevelDark"  Color="#000000" Opacity="0.45"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="BevelLit"   Color="#FFFFFF" Opacity="0.09"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="EdgeLit"    Color="#FFFFFF" Opacity="0.14"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="GlassLo"    Color="#FFFFFF" Opacity="0.035"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="GlassMid"   Color="#FFFFFF" Opacity="0.055"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="GlassHi"    Color="#FFFFFF" Opacity="0.075"/>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SelBg"     Color="#232329"/>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HoverBg"   Color="#17171C"/>

    <!-- ================== TYPE ==================
         SEGOE UI VARIABLE, AND WHY IT IS THREE FAMILIES.
         It is Windows 11's own optically-sized family and the direct counterpart
         to SF Pro and Roboto - which is what "make it look like Apple or Google"
         actually means on this platform. Optical sizing means the letterforms
         are DRAWN differently per size rather than scaled: Small has open
         spacing and sturdy strokes so 11px captions stay legible, Display is
         tightly spaced so headings do not look gappy. Using one face at every
         size is precisely what made this window read as utilitarian.

           Small    <= 12px   captions, overlines, meta
           Text     13-17px   everything you read
           Display  >= 18px   the window title and headings

         Each falls back to plain Segoe UI, so a machine without the variable
         family degrades to what this file used before rather than to Arial.

         🔴 EVERY REFERENCE TO THESE FOUR KEYS IS A DynamicResource, AND THAT IS
         NOT A STYLE PREFERENCE. Install-SRTypeface swaps the shipped Manrope in
         by REPLACING these entries at runtime, after this file has been parsed.
         A {StaticResource FontText} inside a Setter is resolved while the
         dictionary is being built, so it captures the FontFamily object that is
         here NOW and never looks at the key again: the swap then changed the
         key and not one single Style, and the whole window carried on rendering
         Segoe. It looked convincing from a screenshot, and the font test passed,
         because the test asked the KEY what it resolved to rather than asking a
         Style what it was drawn with - and the transcript, which resolves its
         face in script after the swap, really was on Manrope. DynamicResource
         keeps the reference live, so replacing the entry reaches all 15 styles.
         The gui2 suite now asserts this from a realised TextBlock.
         ========================================== -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="FontSmall">Segoe UI Variable Small, Segoe UI</FontFamily>
    <FontFamily x:Key="FontText">Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI</FontFamily>
    <FontFamily x:Key="FontDisplay">Segoe UI Variable Display, Segoe UI</FontFamily>
    <FontFamily x:Key="FontMono">Cascadia Mono, Consolas, Courier New</FontFamily>

    <!-- ================== THE TYPE SCALE - SIX SIZES, AND THAT IS ALL ==========
         🔴 THE CLAIM ABOVE ("one scale, every size comes from it") WAS FALSE
         WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN AND STAYED FALSE. Counted: EIGHT sizes in this file's
         styles (11, 12, 13, 13.5, 14, 15, 17, 20), another eight inline on
         individual elements (10, 11, 12, 13, 13.5, 14, 17), and eight more in
         the document builder (9, 9.5, 10.5, 11.5, 12, 12.5, 13.5) - about
         SIXTEEN distinct sizes, many of them half a pixel apart. Half a pixel is
         too small to read as hierarchy and just big enough to read as a mistake,
         which is exactly what was reported: "not uniform in size ... it looks
         very weird". A scale you have to maintain by hand is not a scale.
         These six ARE the scale now, they are Doubles rather than literals, and
         every FontSize in this file and in the builder points at one of them.

         🔑 MONO IS SIZED BY X-HEIGHT, NOT BY ITS NOMINAL NUMBER. Measured on
         this machine: Manrope's x-height is 0.540 em, Cascadia Mono's is 0.518.
         So the same nominal size draws Cascadia visibly SMALLER, and matching
         the terminal's nominal 16px drew it 28% LARGER than the prose beside it
         (8.28px of x-height against 6.48px) - the single biggest reason the
         voices looked like different documents. 12.5 against a 12 body puts the
         two x-heights within a tenth of a pixel of each other. If either face
         ever changes, this number is derived and must be recomputed, not nudged.

         🪤 THESE ARE OVERWRITTEN AT RUNTIME BY THE ZOOM, so they must be
         DynamicResource at every use site. A StaticResource here captures the
         12 below and never looks again - the same trap the FontFamily comment
         above records, and it would make the zoom silently do nothing.
         ======================================================================= -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="SzMicro">9.5</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="SzCaption">11</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="SzBody">12</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="SzMono">12.5</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="SzStrong">14</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="SzDisplay">17</sys:Double>

    <!-- ================== AND ONE SIZE FOR THE TRANSCRIPT ====================
         🔴 THE READING PANE IS OFF THE SCALE ON PURPOSE, and it is the only
         thing in this file that is. The scale above sizes the CHROME, where a
         hierarchy is the point: a heading outranks a row, a row outranks its
         caption. A transcript has no such hierarchy. It is one column of
         things that were said, and drawing a tool name at 10, its argument at
         12.5, a notice caption at 9.5 and prose at 12 made twelve sizes out of
         one conversation - reported, twice, as "not unified in size".

         So every category of message is drawn at SzPane, in FontPane, full
         stop. No step for labels, none for code, none for tool output. What
         separates a label from prose is its hue and its uppercase, which is
         what a terminal uses too and is enough.

         🪤 THE ZOOM STILL MOVES IT. This is one number, not a fixed one -
         Set-SRTypeScale multiplies it like every other step, so the pane zoom
         keeps working. "One size" means one size AT A GIVEN ZOOM. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="SzPane">13</sys:Double>
    <!-- The transcript's face. IBM Plex Mono ships with the tool (lib\fonts\,
         SIL OFL) and is loaded privately at startup; the stack behind it is
         what draws if the file is ever missing. Humanist rather than
         terminal-drawn, which is the whole reason it is not Cascadia. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="FontPane">IBM Plex Mono, Cascadia Mono, Consolas, Courier New</FontFamily>
    <!-- There is no FontUi alias any more. It existed as a bridge during the
         rebuild and became the way two styles quietly stayed off the scale -
         the buttons and the surface switch were still at 12px while everything
         else moved to 13.5. Three families, one scale, no aliases. -->

    <!-- 🔴 THE WINDOW'S OWN DEFAULT, so anything built in CODE inherits it too.
         The option buttons under a question, the cast rows and the manager menu
         are all created in PowerShell with no style of their own; without this
         they fall back to WPF's default, which is 12px Segoe UI - the one thing
         on screen that would still look like a different application. -->
    <Style TargetType="{x:Type Window}">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
    </Style>
    <!-- ================== RIGHT-CLICK, AND HINTS ==================
         🔴 SETTING THE FONT ON THESE WAS NEVER ENOUGH. A ContextMenu with no
         TEMPLATE keeps the operating system's own chrome: a white slab with a
         blue highlight and a 3D border, thrown up in the middle of a black
         window the moment you right-click a session. Same for ToolTip - a pale
         yellow-white box with a hairline. They are the last two surfaces in the
         tool still drawn by Windows rather than by this file, and they are
         reached constantly. Templated here, on the same tokens as everything
         else, so the design holds all the way through.
         ============================================================ -->
    <Style TargetType="{x:Type ContextMenu}">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextHigh}"/>
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0,6,0,6"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="{x:Type ContextMenu}">
            <Border x:Name="shell" Background="{StaticResource Sheet}" CornerRadius="10"
                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource EdgeLit}" BorderThickness="1"
                    Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}" MinWidth="210"
                    SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
              <Border.Effect>
                <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="28" ShadowDepth="6" Opacity="0.62" Color="#000000"/>
              </Border.Effect>
              <StackPanel IsItemsHost="True" KeyboardNavigation.DirectionalNavigation="Cycle"/>
            </Border>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="{x:Type MenuItem}">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextHigh}"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="{x:Type MenuItem}">
            <Border x:Name="b" Background="Transparent" CornerRadius="7"
                    Margin="6,1,6,1" Padding="12,8,12,8" SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
              <ContentPresenter ContentSource="Header" RecognizesAccessKey="True"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsHighlighted" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SelBg}"/>
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMax}"/>
              </Trigger>
              <!-- A separator is written as a header-less item in the script; it
                   must not look like something you can press. -->
              <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextLow}"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="{x:Type Separator}">
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="{x:Type Separator}">
            <Border Height="1" Margin="12,5,12,5" Background="{StaticResource Hairline}"/>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="{x:Type ToolTip}">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextHigh}"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="{x:Type ToolTip}">
            <Border Background="{StaticResource Sheet}" CornerRadius="8"
                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource EdgeLit}" BorderThickness="1"
                    Padding="11,7,11,8" MaxWidth="380" SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
              <Border.Effect>
                <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="20" ShadowDepth="4" Opacity="0.5" Color="#000000"/>
              </Border.Effect>
              <ContentPresenter/>
            </Border>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ONE SCALE, and every size in the window comes from it. The old file had
         fourteen inline FontSize values between 10.5 and 12.5, which is not a
         scale, it is fourteen decisions. -->
    <Style x:Key="Display" TargetType="TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontDisplay}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzDisplay}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMax}"/>
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="H1" TargetType="TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzStrong}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMax}"/>
    </Style>
    <!-- An overline, not a heading: small, spaced out, quiet. The letter spacing
         is what stops an all-caps run reading as shouting. -->
    <Style x:Key="Caption" TargetType="TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontSmall}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzCaption}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextLow}"/>
      <!-- 🪤 THE ONE PLACE Ideal HAD A SECOND REASON, and it is worth naming
           because it is not the same reason as the window-wide setting. These
           captions are TRACKED - Get-TrackedText builds them by joining each
           character with a THIN SPACE (U+2009), about 1.9px at this size - and
           Ideal kept that fractional advance intact. Display snaps it to 2px.
           Checked in a render at 9.5px: the tracking survives, because a thin
           space rounding 1.9 -> 2.0 is a tenth of a pixel per gap and the
           letters were never on the grid to begin with. Consistency wins; if a
           caption ever looks wrong, this is the line to suspect. -->
      <Setter Property="TextOptions.TextFormattingMode" Value="Display"/>
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="Body" TargetType="TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextHigh}"/>
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="Dim" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource Body}">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMid}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="Meta" TargetType="TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontSmall}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzCaption}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextLow}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ================== SCROLLBAR ==================
         Thin, dark, and out of the way. A default WPF scrollbar is the single
         loudest thing on a dark surface. -->
    <Style TargetType="ScrollBar">
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="9"/>
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="ScrollBar">
            <Grid Background="Transparent">
              <Track x:Name="PART_Track" IsDirectionReversed="True">
                <Track.Thumb>
                  <Thumb>
                    <Thumb.Template>
                      <ControlTemplate TargetType="Thumb">
                        <Border CornerRadius="4" Margin="2,0,2,0" Background="#33383F"/>
                      </ControlTemplate>
                    </Thumb.Template>
                  </Thumb>
                </Track.Thumb>
                <Track.IncreaseRepeatButton>
                  <RepeatButton Command="ScrollBar.PageDownCommand" Opacity="0" Focusable="False"/>
                </Track.IncreaseRepeatButton>
                <Track.DecreaseRepeatButton>
                  <RepeatButton Command="ScrollBar.PageUpCommand" Opacity="0" Focusable="False"/>
                </Track.DecreaseRepeatButton>
              </Track>
            </Grid>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ================== DROPDOWN ==================
         WPF's stock ComboBox is Windows-95 grey with a 3D bevel, which on a dark
         surface is the loudest thing on the screen. Retemplated to match the
         buttons; the popup is retemplated too, or the LIST stays grey even when
         the box does not. -->
    <Style x:Key="DropItem" TargetType="ComboBoxItem">
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,7,10,7"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextHigh}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="ComboBoxItem">
            <Border x:Name="b" Background="Transparent" Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
              <ContentPresenter/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsHighlighted" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SelBg}"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="Drop" TargetType="ComboBox">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextHigh}"/>
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="32"/>
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
      <Setter Property="ItemContainerStyle" Value="{StaticResource DropItem}"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="ComboBox">
            <Grid>
              <ToggleButton x:Name="tb" Focusable="False" ClickMode="Press"
                            IsChecked="{Binding IsDropDownOpen, Mode=TwoWay, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}">
                <ToggleButton.Template>
                  <ControlTemplate TargetType="ToggleButton">
                    <Border x:Name="bb" CornerRadius="7" Background="{StaticResource Ink}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1">
                      <Grid>
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="0" Margin="11,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="&#xE70D;" FontFamily="Segoe MDL2 Assets"
                                   FontSize="{DynamicResource SzMicro}" Foreground="{StaticResource TextLow}"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="8,0,11,0"/>
                      </Grid>
                    </Border>
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                      <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                        <Setter TargetName="bb" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource TextLow}"/>
                      </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                  </ControlTemplate>
                </ToggleButton.Template>
              </ToggleButton>
              <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding SelectionBoxItem}"
                                Margin="11,0,30,0" VerticalAlignment="Center" IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
              <Popup IsOpen="{TemplateBinding IsDropDownOpen}" Placement="Bottom"
                     AllowsTransparency="True" PopupAnimation="Fade" Focusable="False">
                <Border Background="{StaticResource PanelHi}" CornerRadius="8" Margin="0,4,0,0"
                        BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1"
                        MinWidth="{TemplateBinding ActualWidth}" MaxHeight="280">
                  <Border.Effect>
                    <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="18" ShadowDepth="4" Opacity="0.5" Color="#000000"/>
                  </Border.Effect>
                  <ScrollViewer><ItemsPresenter/></ScrollViewer>
                </Border>
              </Popup>
            </Grid>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ================== CHECKBOX ==================
         A filled square, matching the manager's tick: one shape for "this is on"
         everywhere in the window. -->
    <Style x:Key="Check" TargetType="CheckBox">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextHigh}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="CheckBox">
            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Background="Transparent">
              <Border Width="15" Height="15" CornerRadius="3" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                      BorderBrush="#3A3F47" BorderThickness="1.4" Margin="0,0,9,0">
                <Border x:Name="fill" Background="{StaticResource TextMax}" CornerRadius="1.5"
                        Margin="2" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
              </Border>
              <ContentPresenter VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            </StackPanel>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="fill" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ================== CAPTION BUTTONS ==================
         46x46 hit targets, flush to the top-right corner with no margin, so the
         maximised window keeps the Fitts's-law corner the OS gave up. Segoe MDL2
         Assets is the shell's own icon font and is present on every Windows 10
         and 11 machine. -->
    <Style x:Key="CaptionBtn" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="46"/>
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="Segoe MDL2 Assets"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzCaption}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMid}"/>
      <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
            <Border x:Name="b" Background="Transparent">
              <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="Background" Value="#23262C"/>
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMax}"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="CaptionBtnClose" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource CaptionBtn}">
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
            <Border x:Name="b" Background="Transparent">
              <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="Background" Value="#C42B1C"/>
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#FFFFFF"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ================== BUTTONS ================== -->
    <Style x:Key="Btn" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextHigh}"/>
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="11,6,11,6"/>
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
            <Border x:Name="b" CornerRadius="6" Background="{StaticResource PanelHi}"
                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1"
                    Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
              <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="Background" Value="#23262C"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Focus}"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.4"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- 🪤 ITS OWN STYLE, NOT A SMALLER Btn. Btn is also the top bar's New
         session / Rescan / Send to many and the footer's Save, which are
         primary actions and should not shrink with these. These six sit under
         a title as secondary controls: same shape, one step quieter. -->
    <Style x:Key="BtnSlim" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource Btn}">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMid}"/>
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="9,3,9,4"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- An answer to a question, not a control on a toolbar. It is a full-width
         row carrying a label and the reasoning under it, so it needs a taller
         ground, a left alignment and room for two lines of type - and it sits
         INSIDE the question panel, so its ground is a step up from that panel
         rather than the window's raised surface. -->
    <Style x:Key="BtnOption" TargetType="Button">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMax}"/>
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="15,11,15,12"/>
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
            <Border x:Name="b" CornerRadius="11" Background="{StaticResource GlassMid}"
                    BorderBrush="Transparent" BorderThickness="1"
                    Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}" SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
              <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource GlassHi}"/>
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource HueAskEdge}"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource HueAsk}"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.4"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="BtnPrimary" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource Btn}">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="#141518"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
            <Border x:Name="b" CornerRadius="6" Background="{StaticResource AccNeeds}"
                    Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
              <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="Opacity" Value="0.9"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.35"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- The surface switch. Two segments, and the CHECKED one is filled: the
         two surfaces differ by shape, so the switch has to say plainly which
         shape you are looking at. -->
    <Style x:Key="Seg" TargetType="RadioButton">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMid}"/>
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="13,6,13,6"/>
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="RadioButton">
            <Border x:Name="b" CornerRadius="5" Background="Transparent"
                    Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
              <ContentPresenter HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SelBg}"/>
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMax}"/>
                <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextHigh}"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Focus}"/>
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="BorderThickness" Value="1"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ================== SEARCH BOX ================== -->
    <Style x:Key="Search" TargetType="TextBox">
      <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{DynamicResource FontText}"/>
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource SzBody}"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextMax}"/>
      <Setter Property="CaretBrush" Value="{StaticResource AccNeeds}"/>
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,0,12,0"/>
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="30"/>
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="TextBox">
            <Border x:Name="b" CornerRadius="8" Background="{StaticResource Ink}"
                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1">
              <Grid>
                <!-- THE PLACEHOLDER. An empty rounded rectangle says nothing
                     about what it is for; the search box read as decoration.
                     🔴 THE TEXT COMES FROM THE BOX'S OWN Tag, never from this
                     style: the composer uses this same style, and a hard-coded
                     string put "Search conversations..." inside the box you type
                     replies into. Shown only while empty, and not hit-testable so
                     it can never eat a click meant for the field beneath. -->
                <TextBlock x:Name="ph"
                           Text="{Binding RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}, Path=Tag}"
                           FontFamily="{DynamicResource FontText}" FontSize="{DynamicResource SzBody}"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextLow}" IsHitTestVisible="False"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                           Visibility="Collapsed"/>
                <!-- Padding applied ONCE - see the note in the Composer style
                     below. Here it put the typed text 12px right of the
                     placeholder it replaces; invisible in daily use only
                     because the placeholder vanishes on the first keystroke. -->
                <ScrollViewer x:Name="PART_ContentHost" Margin="0"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
              </Grid>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="Text" Value="">
                <Setter TargetName="ph" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocusWithin" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Focus}"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ================== THE COMPOSER ==================
         🔴 A BOX YOU TYPE A REPLY INTO IS NOT A SEARCH BOX, and sharing the
         Search style is what made it unusable. Search pins Height="30" and
         centres PART_ContentHost, so the composer was physically incapable of
         growing: with AcceptsReturn="False" and no wrapping, everything past
         one line's width scrolled out of sight to the left. Reported as "the
         text is cut off and I cannot see my full reply".

         So this is Search's look with the three properties that made it a
         single line reversed: no fixed Height (MinHeight instead), a content
         host that stretches rather than centres, and wrapping on. It grows
         with what you type and only starts scrolling at MaxHeight, so what is
         on screen is always the END of what you wrote - the part you are
         still typing.

         🪤 MaxHeight, not unbounded. The composer sits in an Auto row at the
         foot of the pane, and a box that grows without limit would push the
         transcript off the top of the window on a long message. 168px is
         about seven lines; past that it scrolls. -->
    <Style x:Key="Composer" TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource Search}">
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="NaN"/>
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="30"/>
      <Setter Property="MaxHeight" Value="168"/>
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,6,12,7"/>
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Top"/>
      <Setter Property="AcceptsReturn" Value="True"/>
      <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap"/>
      <Setter Property="VerticalScrollBarVisibility" Value="Auto"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="TextBox">
            <Border x:Name="b" CornerRadius="8" Background="{StaticResource Ink}"
                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1">
              <Grid>
                <TextBlock x:Name="ph"
                           Text="{Binding RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}, Path=Tag}"
                           FontFamily="{DynamicResource FontText}" FontSize="{DynamicResource SzBody}"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextLow}" IsHitTestVisible="False"
                           TextWrapping="NoWrap" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                           Visibility="Collapsed"/>
                <!-- 🔴 THE PADDING IS APPLIED ONCE, HERE, BY THE TEXTBOX ITSELF.
                     Measured: with Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}" on this
                     ScrollViewer the caret sat at (26.4, 13.6) against a
                     placeholder at (12.8, 7.2) - almost exactly one padding out
                     in each axis, because the inset was applied twice: once as
                     this margin and again by the TextBox's own text view, which
                     honours Padding regardless. Reported as "the cursor when
                     nothing is entered is misaligned with the background
                     message". No margin here; the placeholder takes the same
                     Padding by TemplateBinding so the two cannot drift apart
                     again if it is ever retuned. -->
                <ScrollViewer x:Name="PART_ContentHost" Margin="0"
                              VerticalAlignment="Stretch"/>
              </Grid>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="Text" Value="">
                <Setter TargetName="ph" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocusWithin" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource Focus}"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- ================== LIST BASE ==================
         Virtualizing and recycling on purpose: the sessions column can hold
         every conversation in the registry, and a ListBox that realises 214
         two-line rows is a visible stall on every rebuild. -->
    <Style x:Key="Plain" TargetType="ListBox">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
      <Setter Property="ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility" Value="Disabled"/>
      <Setter Property="VirtualizingPanel.IsVirtualizing" Value="True"/>
      <Setter Property="VirtualizingPanel.VirtualizationMode" Value="Recycling"/>
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch"/>
      <Setter Property="KeyboardNavigation.DirectionalNavigation" Value="Contained"/>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="PlainItem" TargetType="ListBoxItem">
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0"/>
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch"/>
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <Setter.Value>
          <ControlTemplate TargetType="ListBoxItem">
            <Border x:Name="b" Background="Transparent" Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
              <ContentPresenter/>
            </Border>
            <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
              <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource HoverBg}"/>
              </Trigger>
              <Trigger Property="IsSelected" Value="True">
                <Setter TargetName="b" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SelBg}"/>
              </Trigger>
            </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
          </ControlTemplate>
        </Setter.Value>
      </Setter>
    </Style>


    <!-- ================== THE SESSION ROW ==================
         TWO LINES, because the one thing that makes this list scannable is what
         each conversation last SAID, and a 336px column cannot hold that on the
         same line as a name. Line one is identity and age; line two is the last
         thing it said, ellipsised.

         The band accent appears as a 3px bar down the left of a row and as a dot
         beside the name - never as the only signal. The band HEADING above it
         says the same thing in words, so the grouping survives without colour.

         Visibility is a property ON THE ROW OBJECT rather than a converter:
         PowerShell can set System.Windows.Visibility directly and it binds with
         no extra type in the way.
         ==================================================== -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="SessionRowTpl">
      <Grid>
        <!-- the band heading -->
        <Border Visibility="{Binding BandVis}" Padding="12,10,12,8" Margin="6,8,6,0"
                CornerRadius="8" Background="{Binding BandBg}" Cursor="Hand"
                ToolTip="Show only these - click again for all of them">
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
            <Border Width="3" Height="13" CornerRadius="2" Background="{Binding Accent}"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,9,0"/>
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding BandLabel}" Style="{StaticResource Caption}"
                       Foreground="{Binding Accent}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding BandCount}" Style="{StaticResource Caption}"
                       FontWeight="Normal" Margin="9,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding BandHint}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                       Margin="10,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- a conversation
             🔴 TWO MARKS, EARNED. The list was asked to get LESS dense, so
             neither of these is on a row that has nothing to say: the context
             bar appears once a conversation is past half its window, and the
             sub-agent pill only while one is actually out. On a quiet morning
             this row is exactly what it was before, which is the point - a
             signal that is always on has spent density and bought nothing. -->
        <Border Visibility="{Binding RowVis}" Padding="0,12,14,13">
          <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="3"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <Border Grid.Column="0" Background="{Binding Accent}" Opacity="{Binding BarOpacity}"/>
            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Margin="15,0,0,0">
              <Grid>
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <Ellipse Grid.Column="0" Width="6" Height="6" Fill="{Binding Accent}"
                         Visibility="{Binding DotVis}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,8,0"/>
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Name}" Style="{StaticResource Body}"
                           FontWeight="{Binding NameWeight}" FontStyle="{Binding NameStyle}"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextMax}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"/>

                <!-- 🔴 WHAT THIS SESSION HAS OUT, in two shapes that are never
                     the same shape. A sub-agent is a ROUND amber dot: another
                     mind working on your behalf. A background shell is a SQUARE
                     violet mark: machinery still running. A session can have
                     both, so they are two marks and never one total - and the
                     shape, not the hue, is what carries it, so a greyscale
                     screenshot and a colour-blind reader both still see two
                     different things. -->
                <!-- 🪤 THE MARK, NOT A PILL AROUND THE MARK. Each of these was
                     a rounded tinted capsule holding a 5px shape and a number -
                     three pieces of chrome to say a thing one shape already
                     says. On a row whose whole job is to be scanned that reads
                     as a badge competing with the name. The shape carries it
                     alone now, at the size it can actually be seen at, with the
                     count beside it only when there is more than one. -->
                <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Visibility="{Binding AgentVis}"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="10,0,0,0"
                              ToolTip="Sub-agents this conversation has out">
                    <Ellipse Width="7" Height="7" Fill="{StaticResource HueOut}"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding AgentText}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource HueOut}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               Margin="4,0,0,0"/>
                  </StackPanel>
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Visibility="{Binding ShellVis}"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="10,0,0,0"
                              ToolTip="Background commands this conversation still has running">
                    <Rectangle Width="7" Height="7" Fill="{StaticResource HueTool}"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding ShellText}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                               Foreground="{StaticResource HueTool}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               Margin="4,0,0,0"/>
                  </StackPanel>
                  <!-- 🔴 WHAT IS WAITING BEHIND THE TURN IT IS ON. A third
                       shape, for the same reason the two above are two: this
                       is not a sub-agent and not a shell, it is your own words
                       not yet read. The guillemet is not a new invention - the
                       reading pane has used it for queued input since it was
                       written, so the two surfaces say the same thing with the
                       same glyph.
                       🪤 NO PILL. See the note above: a tinted capsule around a
                       count was removed from this row once already for reading
                       as a badge competing with the name, and adding one back
                       here would undo that for the sake of consistency with a
                       mockup rather than with this row.
                       🔴 AND IT GOES GREY WHEN THE QUEUE IS NOT YOURS. Measured
                       on this machine: of everything queued across 432
                       transcripts, 1,356 were cross-session messages and 1,107
                       task notifications against 144 lines a person typed - so
                       a mark that lit up for all of it would be lit almost
                       always and mean nothing. Amber says YOUR words are
                       waiting; grey says the machine is talking to itself. -->
                  <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Visibility="{Binding QVis}"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="10,0,0,0"
                              ToolTip="{Binding QTip}">
                    <TextBlock Text="&#187;" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                               Foreground="{Binding QBrush}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding QText}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                               Foreground="{Binding QBrush}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               Margin="3,0,0,0"/>
                  </StackPanel>
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- how full its context is, once that is worth knowing -->
                <Border Grid.Column="3" Visibility="{Binding CtxVis}" Width="34" Height="4"
                        CornerRadius="2" Background="{StaticResource CtxTrack}"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="10,0,0,0"
                        ToolTip="How much of its context window this conversation has used">
                  <Border Height="4" CornerRadius="2" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                          Width="{Binding CtxWidth}" Background="{Binding CtxBrush}"/>
                </Border>

                <TextBlock Grid.Column="4" Text="{Binding Age}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                           Margin="10,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
              </Grid>
              <!-- The last thing it said. Given its own leading rather than the
                   default, because at 13px with none it sat hard against the
                   name above it and the two read as one block of text. -->
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Said}" Style="{StaticResource Dim}"
                         TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" Margin="0,5,0,0"
                         LineHeight="17" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/>
            </StackPanel>
          </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- ================== A SUB-AGENT ==================
             🔑 A sub-agent is a CONVERSATION, and it is selectable like any
             other. Claude Code writes each one its own transcript beside the
             parent, so opening one is the same act as opening a session - which
             is why this row goes in this list rather than into a panel of its
             own. Measured 2026-08-31: 329 of 374 sub-agents on this machine
             have a full transcript on disk.

             It is INDENTED and it is QUIET. The list is scanned for what needs
             you, and a sub-agent never does - it belongs to the session above
             it. So: no accent bar, no unread dot, no context bar; an @ in the
             agent hue, the name, what it was asked to do, and how long ago it
             last wrote.

             🪤 SubVis, not AgentVis. `AgentVis` already exists on this template
             and means something else entirely - the amber dot counting agents a
             SESSION has out. Two properties one letter apart, on one template,
             would have been a silent empty cell the first time they were
             confused. -->
        <Border Visibility="{Binding SubVis}" Padding="0,7,14,8" Cursor="Hand"
                ToolTip="{Binding SubTip}">
          <Grid Margin="34,0,0,0">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="&#x0040;" FontFamily="{DynamicResource FontMono}"
                       FontSize="{DynamicResource SzCaption}" Foreground="{StaticResource HueAsk}"
                       Opacity="{Binding SubOpacity}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="0,1,9,0"/>
            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding SubName}" Style="{StaticResource Body}"
                           FontSize="{DynamicResource SzBody}" Foreground="{StaticResource TextHigh}"
                           Opacity="{Binding SubOpacity}"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding SubTag}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource TextLow}" Margin="9,0,0,0"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
              </StackPanel>
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding SubDesc}" Style="{StaticResource Dim}"
                         FontSize="{DynamicResource SzBody}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" Margin="0,3,0,0"
                         LineHeight="16" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"/>
            </StackPanel>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding SubAge}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                       Margin="10,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          </Grid>
        </Border>
      </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!-- The rail row: a project, its conversation count, and whether it is the
         one currently filtering. It FILTERS - it is never the grouping. -->
    <!-- ================== A PROJECT TILE ==================
         This was a one-line text row - a name and a number - and at 29
         projects it read as a directory listing rather than as the place you
         choose where to look next. A tile has room for the thing that actually
         decides that: what is happening INSIDE the project. "13" is the same
         number whether all 13 are asleep or one is waiting on you.

         🔴 THE ONLY HUE IN THE WINDOW IS THE 3px BAR, and that is the whole
         concession. Grey carries STATE, hue carries IDENTITY, and they never
         swap jobs: the bar says WHICH project, the greys say how it is doing.
         A tinted surface would put the accent in competition with the white
         that means NEEDS YOU, which is the one thing in this window that must
         never have to compete for attention.
         ================================================== -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="RailRowTpl">
      <Grid>
      <!-- an age band heading: TODAY / THIS WEEK / THIS MONTH / OLDER.
           🪤 THE CARET IS THE AFFORDANCE, not the highlight the sessions
           column uses. That one FILTERS and stays lit while it is on; this one
           FOLDS, and a fold has a direction - a caret says which way the next
           click goes, where a lit background would only say "this is the one
           you pressed" about a band that is now empty on screen. -->
      <Border Visibility="{Binding BandVis}" Padding="12,10,12,6" Margin="6,8,6,0"
              CornerRadius="8" Background="Transparent" Cursor="Hand"
              ToolTip="Fold this group of projects away - click again to bring it back">
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding BandCaret}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,8,0"/>
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding BandLabel}" Style="{StaticResource Caption}"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          <TextBlock Text="{Binding BandCount}" Style="{StaticResource Caption}"
                     FontWeight="Normal" Foreground="{StaticResource TextLow}"
                     Margin="9,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- 🪤 THE SAME RHYTHM THE SESSION ROWS WERE GIVEN. The tile was tuned
           when it was the only two-line thing in the window; beside the rows'
           new spacing it read tight, and a rail that is denser than the list it
           filters gets the emphasis backwards. Nothing here changes what a tile
           SAYS - only how much air it says it in. -->
      <Border Visibility="{Binding RowVis}" ToolTip="{Binding Tip}"
              Margin="8,4,8,4" CornerRadius="9" Background="{Binding PickBg}"
              BorderBrush="{Binding PickEdge}" BorderThickness="1"
              Padding="0,11,12,12" SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
        <Grid>
          <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
          </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
          <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
          </Grid.RowDefinitions>

          <!-- the identity mark -->
          <Border Grid.Column="0" Grid.RowSpan="2" Width="4" CornerRadius="2"
                  Margin="11,1,13,1" Background="{Binding Accent}"
                  Opacity="{Binding AccentOpacity}"/>

          <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Grid.Row="0" Text="{Binding Label}"
                     Style="{StaticResource Body}" Foreground="{Binding Fg}"
                     FontWeight="SemiBold" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"/>
          <!-- The dot repeats what the word says, for the one state worth
               finding without reading: a project with something waiting. -->
          <Border Grid.Column="2" Grid.Row="0" Width="6" Height="6" CornerRadius="3"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="8,0,0,0"
                  Visibility="{Binding NeedsVis}"
                  Background="{StaticResource AccNeeds}"/>
          <!-- What is happening inside. Given its own leading and a real gap
               from the name: at 2px the two lines read as one wrapped title
               rather than as a name and its state. -->
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" Grid.Row="1"
                     Text="{Binding State}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                     Margin="0,4,0,0" LineHeight="15" LineStackingStrategy="BlockLineHeight"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"/>
        </Grid>
      </Border>
      </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>


    <!-- ================== THE MANAGER ROW ==================
         A FULL WIDTH TABLE, and that is the point: the session manager differs
         from the work surface by SHAPE, so the two can never be mistaken for one
         another - in a screenshot, on a monochrome screen, or by a reader who
         cannot tell the accents apart.

         The LOGON tick is the first column and fenced off by a rule, because it
         is the one control on this screen that decides tomorrow morning. It is a
         checkbox and it never launches anything.
         ===================================================== -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="ManageRowTpl">
      <Grid>
        <!-- a project header -->
        <Border Visibility="{Binding ProjVis}" Padding="0,12,20,10" Background="{StaticResource Panel}"
                Cursor="Hand">
          <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="86"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Caret}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,0,10,0"/>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Label}" Style="{StaticResource Body}"
                       FontWeight="SemiBold" Foreground="{StaticResource TextMax}"
                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"/>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding Meta}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"/>
          </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- a conversation -->
        <Border Visibility="{Binding ConvVis}" Padding="0,8,20,8">
          <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="86"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="260"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="180"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <!-- Named so the click handler can tell "clicked the box" from
                 "clicked the row". A wider transparent hit area than the 15px
                 mark, because a checkbox you have to aim at is a checkbox that
                 reads as broken. -->
            <Border Grid.Column="0" x:Name="TickBox" Background="Transparent" Cursor="Hand"
                    ToolTip="Click to tick - a ticked conversation reopens at your next logon">
              <Border Width="15" Height="15" CornerRadius="3" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center" BorderBrush="#3A3F47" BorderThickness="1.4"
                      Background="{Binding TickBg}"/>
            </Border>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Name}" Style="{StaticResource Body}"
                       FontStyle="{Binding NameStyle}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" Margin="14,0,10,0"/>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding Lane}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" Margin="0,0,10,0"/>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="3" Text="{Binding Said}" Style="{StaticResource Dim}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"/>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="4" Text="{Binding Age}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="10,0,0,0"/>
          </Grid>
        </Border>
      </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>

  </Window.Resources>

  <!-- =========================================================================
       THE SHELL — an INSET CARD, not a maximised rectangle.
       The window's own background is the ground; the app is a rounded card
       floating on it with the ground visible around every edge. That gap is the
       single change that makes this read as a designed object rather than a
       window that happens to be dark, and it costs 14px per side.

       Four rows inside it: title bar, surface switch, the body, status.
       The BODY is where the two surfaces differ:
         work surface    tiles | sessions | output pane
         session manager one full-width table
       Only one of the two is ever visible.

       🪤 THE CARD IS CLIPPED, so its rounded corners actually cut the content.
       Without the clip the corners are painted but children square them off
       again, which looks like a rendering bug rather than a radius.
       ========================================================================= -->
  <Border x:Name="Shell" Margin="14" CornerRadius="18" Background="{StaticResource Panel}"
          BorderBrush="{StaticResource EdgeLit}" BorderThickness="1"
          SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
    <Border.Effect>
      <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="46" ShadowDepth="14" Opacity="0.62" Color="#000000"/>
    </Border.Effect>
    <Border.Clip>
      <RectangleGeometry x:Name="ShellClip" RadiusX="17" RadiusY="17" Rect="0,0,1452,952"/>
    </Border.Clip>
  <Grid>
    <Grid.RowDefinitions>
      <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
      <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
      <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
      <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
    </Grid.RowDefinitions>

    <!-- ========== 0. title - the app's OWN caption ==========
         This strip IS the title bar. Dragging it moves the window (DragMove,
         wired in the script); double-clicking it maximises. Everything
         interactive on it carries IsHitTestVisibleInChrome so the chrome does
         not eat the click first. -->
    <Border x:Name="TitleBar" Grid.Row="0" Background="{StaticResource Panel}"
            BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="0,0,0,1" Padding="20,0,0,0"
            Height="46">
      <Grid>
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
          <!-- 🔴 STAR, NOT Auto. An Auto column grows to whatever its content
               wants, so the left group could push the search column below the
               search box's own MinWidth - and a box narrower than its MinWidth is
               still DRAWN at MinWidth, over its neighbours. Star makes the title
               group the thing that yields, which is what its internal trimming is
               there for. -->
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
        <!-- 🔴 A GRID, NOT A StackPanel, AND THE ZOOM IS WHY. A horizontal
             StackPanel gives every child its full desired width and never
             clips, so this group simply GREW past its star column and was drawn
             on top of the search box - the same overlap the MinWidth comment
             below records, arriving from the other side. It was latent while
             every size was fixed; at 150% the text is half again as wide and it
             happens on the first press of the button.
             Auto | * | Auto: the title and the bridge note keep their size, and
             the count - the one part that is a nicety rather than a warning -
             is the thing that gives way. -->
        <Grid Grid.Column="0" VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
          </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Claude sessions" Style="{StaticResource Display}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" x:Name="LiveCount" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" Margin="16,1,8,0"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" Text=""/>
          <!-- 🔴 WHY REMOTE CONTROL HAS GONE QUIET, said out loud. Claude Code
               counts consecutive bridge failures and stops trying on the
               seventh; while that holds, sessions run perfectly and simply do
               not register, so the phone shows nothing and it reads as being
               signed out. Invisible until now - which is why signing in every
               morning appeared to fix it. Read-only: this tool does not own
               ~/.claude.json and does not write it.
               🪤 ON THE LEFT, WITH THE OTHER GLOBAL FACT. It sat in the
               right-hand group first and OVERLAPPED THE SEARCH BOX: Search
               carries MinWidth 380 and centres itself in a star column, so it
               spills over its neighbour the moment that group grows. This is
               also simply where it belongs - it describes the machine, not the
               buttons. -->
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" x:Name="BridgeNote" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Margin="0,1,0,0"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" Visibility="Collapsed"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                     ToolTip="Remote Control registration health, read from Claude Code's own state"/>
        </Grid>
        <!-- 🪤 MinWidth WAS 380 AND THAT IS WHAT OVERLAPPED THE HEADER. Centred
             in a star column, a box whose MinWidth exceeds the space the column
             was actually given still renders at MinWidth, and spills over BOTH
             sides - so the moment either group beside it grew, the search box
             was drawn on top of them. That is the header overlap reported
             twice, and it was latent until something was added beside it. 260
             lets the column genuinely shrink; MaxWidth keeps it generous when
             there is room. -->
        <!-- 🔴 Stretch, NOT Center - THAT IS THE ACTUAL FIX, and lowering the
             MinWidth to 260 only moved the threshold. A CENTRED element asks
             for its MinWidth and, when the column is narrower than that, is
             drawn at MinWidth anyway, spilling equally over BOTH neighbours. A
             STRETCHED one is given exactly the column, so it cannot overflow at
             any size; MaxWidth still keeps it centred and generous when there
             is room, which is the look this had before. Measured by the header
             assertion in gui2 at 150%, where the centred box overlapped the
             clock and the bridge note by 84px each. -->
        <TextBox x:Name="Search" Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource Search}"
                 Tag="Search conversations, projects, worktrees"
                 MaxWidth="560" MinWidth="0" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" Margin="12,0,12,0"
                 shell:WindowChrome.IsHitTestVisibleInChrome="True"
                 ToolTip="Find by title, id, worktree or project. Press / from anywhere."/>
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    shell:WindowChrome.IsHitTestVisibleInChrome="True" Margin="0,0,10,0">
          <TextBlock x:Name="Stamp" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Margin="0,0,14,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
          <Button x:Name="NewSession" Content="New session" Style="{StaticResource BtnPrimary}" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                  ToolTip="Start a fresh conversation (Ctrl+N)"/>
          <!-- 🔑 SIGN IN, THEN BRING THE SESSIONS BACK. A running claude reads
               the login token AT STARTUP, so signing in does not reach anything
               already open - which is why re-logging in was always followed by
               relaunching everything by hand. One button does both. -->
          <Button x:Name="SignIn" Content="Sign in" Style="{StaticResource Btn}" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                  ToolTip="Open a terminal to sign in, then relaunch the ticked conversations onto the new login"/>
          <Button x:Name="Rescan" Content="Rescan" Style="{StaticResource Btn}"/>
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- The window buttons. Close goes red on hover the way every Windows
             app does; the other two lighten. Symbols are Segoe MDL2 glyphs, the
             same ones the OS caption uses, so they read as window controls
             rather than as letters. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="3" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Stretch"
                    shell:WindowChrome.IsHitTestVisibleInChrome="True">
          <Button x:Name="WinMin" Style="{StaticResource CaptionBtn}" Content="&#xE921;"
                  ToolTip="Minimise" AutomationProperties.Name="Minimise"/>
          <Button x:Name="WinMax" Style="{StaticResource CaptionBtn}" Content="&#xE922;"
                  ToolTip="Maximise" AutomationProperties.Name="Maximise"/>
          <Button x:Name="WinClose" Style="{StaticResource CaptionBtnClose}" Content="&#xE8BB;"
                  ToolTip="Close" AutomationProperties.Name="Close"/>
        </StackPanel>
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <!-- ========== 1. which surface ========== -->
    <Border Grid.Row="1" Background="{StaticResource Ink}"
            BorderBrush="{StaticResource HairSoft}" BorderThickness="0,0,0,1" Padding="18,9,18,9">
      <Grid>
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
        <Border Grid.Column="0" Background="{StaticResource Panel}" CornerRadius="7"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1" Padding="3">
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
            <RadioButton x:Name="ModeWork" Style="{StaticResource Seg}" Content="Work surface" IsChecked="True"
                         ToolTip="What is happening now: every conversation that is running or was, grouped by what it needs from you."/>
            <RadioButton x:Name="ModeManage" Style="{StaticResource Seg}" Content="Session manager"
                         ToolTip="What comes back at logon: every conversation by project, with the tick that decides."/>
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>
        <!-- Column 1 carried a row of band chips that was never populated, and
             stayed empty on purpose: the band headings inside the list already
             show every count, and duplicating them in the chrome was the thing
             the redesign set out to remove.

             These two are not that. They are VIEW controls - the only place in
             the window that says how much room the reading pane has - and they
             live here rather than in the top bar because that bar has already
             overlapped itself twice when something was added beside the search
             box. Their labels say what pressing them DOES, not what is
             currently true, which is the one thing a toggle must never get
             backwards. -->
        <!-- 🪤 THE TWO FOLD BUTTONS THAT WERE HERE ARE GONE, NOT MOVED-AND-KEPT.
             They were the first attempt and the operator could not find them;
             the carets now live on the columns' own headers, which is where the
             looking happens. Leaving both would be two controls for one action
             and would spend header width that has already overlapped twice. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <Button x:Name="Broadcast" Content="Send to many" Style="{StaticResource Btn}"/>
        </StackPanel>
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <!-- ========== 2. the body ========== -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="2">

      <!-- ===== WORK SURFACE: rail | sessions | output pane =====
           The splitters are real, and their widths are remembered. The rail and
           the sessions column collapse on narrow windows: see Set-Breakpoint in
           the script. Three columns is not a luxury that vanishes when the
           window is small, it is a layout with a defined behaviour at every
           width. -->
      <!-- 🔴 THE PANES ARE CARDS ON A GROUND, not regions divided by hairlines.
           They were flat rectangles separated by 1px rules, which is what the
           operator meant by "the rounded edges and backgrounds are all gone" -
           only the OUTER shell had ever been given the treatment the canvas
           showed. Each pane now has its own radius, its own edge and its own
           shadow, and the gutters between them are the ground showing through.
           That is the whole difference between a window that reads as layered
           and one that reads as partitioned. -->
      <Grid x:Name="WorkSurface" Background="{StaticResource Ink}">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
          <ColumnDefinition x:Name="RailCol" Width="248" MinWidth="0"/>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
          <ColumnDefinition x:Name="ListCol" Width="372" MinWidth="0"/>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*" MinWidth="280"/>
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <!-- the projects rail: a FILTER, never the grouping -->
        <Border x:Name="RailPane" Grid.Column="0" Background="{StaticResource Panel}"
                CornerRadius="12" Margin="10,4,3,10" SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1">
          <Border.Effect>
            <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="18" ShadowDepth="3" Opacity="0.42" Color="#000000"/>
          </Border.Effect>
          <Grid>
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
              <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
              <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <!-- 🔴 THE PANE SEARCHES, SORTS AND FILTERS ITSELF. The header box
                 is the GLOBAL one and reaches both panes at once; that is not
                 the same as narrowing this list while leaving the other alone,
                 which is what the operator asked for twice. -->
            <StackPanel Grid.Row="0">
              <Border Padding="16,12,12,6">
                <Grid>
                  <TextBlock Text="PROJECTS" Style="{StaticResource Caption}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                  <!-- 🔴 THE CONTROL SITS ON THE THING IT COLLAPSES. It was a
                       pair of text buttons in the header row and the operator
                       could not find them - "I still do not see the collapse
                       buttons" - which for a control is the same as not having
                       one. A caret in the column's own header needs no label to
                       be understood, and it is where you look. -->
                  <TextBlock x:Name="RailFold" Text="&#x2039;" Cursor="Hand"
                             Style="{StaticResource Caption}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,0,2,0" Padding="6,0,6,0"
                             ToolTip="Collapse the projects column (Ctrl+1)"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="collapse the projects column"/>
                  <TextBlock x:Name="RailClear" Text="clear" Style="{StaticResource Dim}"
                             HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             Cursor="Hand" Visibility="Collapsed"
                             ToolTip="Stop filtering by project."/>
                </Grid>
              </Border>
              <TextBox x:Name="RailSearch" Style="{StaticResource Search}"
                       Margin="12,0,12,7" Tag="Find a project"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="find a project"/>
              <Border Padding="14,0,12,9">
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                  <TextBlock x:Name="RailSort" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Cursor="Hand"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             ToolTip="Change how the projects are ordered"/>
                  <TextBlock Text="  ·  " Style="{StaticResource Meta}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                  <TextBlock x:Name="RailOnlyLive" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Cursor="Hand"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             ToolTip="Show only projects with something running"/>
                  <!-- 🔴 HOW MANY ARE SHELVED, never silence. Shelving a project
                       takes it off this rail AND out of the logon restore, so a
                       rail that simply drew fewer tiles would be a rail whose
                       length lies. It appears only when something is shelved -
                       see Update-RailShelved - because "0 shelved" is chrome
                       explaining a state nobody is in.
                       🪤 NOT the session-level "hidden", which is a launch flag
                       meaning "open this console off-screen". Different object,
                       different word, on purpose. -->
                  <TextBlock x:Name="RailShelved" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Cursor="Hand"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="10,0,0,0"
                             Visibility="Collapsed"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="show shelved projects"/>
                </StackPanel>
              </Border>
              <!-- 🔴 A LINE OF ITS OWN, AND IT IS NOT A CONTROL. The rail shows
                   only what was touched in the last day, so a project quiet for
                   a fortnight is never a tile here and there is nowhere on one
                   to mark it - the count has to be able to speak for projects
                   the rail cannot draw. It has no Cursor and no click: the tool
                   points at what could be put away and never puts anything
                   away, so a control that looked pressable would be promising
                   an action that deliberately does not exist. The names are in
                   the tooltip; hiding is the right-click on the tile. -->
              <TextBlock x:Name="RailSuggest" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                         Margin="14,0,12,9" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Visibility="Collapsed"
                         AutomationProperties.Name="projects that could be shelved"/>
            </StackPanel>
            <ListBox x:Name="RailList" Grid.Row="1" Style="{StaticResource Plain}"
                     ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource PlainItem}"
                     ItemTemplate="{StaticResource RailRowTpl}"
                     AutomationProperties.Name="projects"/>
          </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- ============ THE PROJECTS COLUMN, COLLAPSED ============
             🪤 NOT TO ZERO, TO A CARET. The rail is a filter and carries no
             state worth keeping on screen, so almost all of it goes - but a
             control that collapses its own column and leaves nothing behind
             has removed the only way back, and the keyboard shortcut is not
             discoverable enough to be the answer. 26px is the caret and
             nothing else. -->
        <Border x:Name="RailStrip" Grid.Column="0" Visibility="Collapsed"
                Background="{StaticResource Panel}" CornerRadius="12" Margin="10,4,3,10"
                SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1">
          <TextBlock x:Name="RailOpen" Text="&#x203A;" Cursor="Hand"
                     Style="{StaticResource Caption}"
                     HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                     Margin="0,13,0,0" Padding="6,2,6,2"
                     ToolTip="Show the projects column again (Ctrl+1)"
                     AutomationProperties.Name="show the projects column"/>
        </Border>

        <GridSplitter x:Name="RailSplit" Grid.Column="1" Width="4" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                      Background="Transparent" ResizeBehavior="PreviousAndNext"/>

        <!-- the sessions column: grouped by BAND, two lines per row -->
        <Border x:Name="ListPane" Grid.Column="2" Background="{StaticResource Panel}"
                CornerRadius="12" Margin="3,4,3,10" SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1">
          <Border.Effect>
            <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="18" ShadowDepth="3" Opacity="0.42" Color="#000000"/>
          </Border.Effect>
          <Grid>
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
              <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
              <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
              <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <Border Grid.Row="0" Padding="16,12,12,6">
              <Grid>
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <TextBlock x:Name="ListCaption" Text="SESSIONS" Style="{StaticResource Caption}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                  <!-- The order inside each band. A label rather than a dropdown:
                       there are three of them and cycling is one click. -->
                  <TextBlock x:Name="ListSort" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Cursor="Hand"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="12,0,0,0"
                             ToolTip="Change the order inside each band"/>
                </StackPanel>
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <TextBlock x:Name="ListCount" Style="{StaticResource Dim}"
                             VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                  <!-- Same caret, same reason - see the projects header. -->
                  <TextBlock x:Name="ListFold" Text="&#x2039;" Cursor="Hand"
                             Style="{StaticResource Caption}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             Margin="8,0,2,0" Padding="6,0,6,0"
                             ToolTip="Collapse the sessions column to a strip of marks (Ctrl+2)"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="collapse the sessions column"/>
                </StackPanel>
              </Grid>
            </Border>
            <!-- This pane's own search, independent of the header's global one. -->
            <TextBox x:Name="ListSearch" Grid.Row="1" Style="{StaticResource Search}"
                     Margin="12,0,12,8" Tag="Find a conversation"
                     AutomationProperties.Name="find a conversation"/>
            <ListBox x:Name="SessionList" Grid.Row="2" Style="{StaticResource Plain}"
                     ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource PlainItem}"
                     ItemTemplate="{StaticResource SessionRowTpl}"
                     AutomationProperties.Name="sessions"/>
          </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- ============ THE SESSIONS COLUMN, COLLAPSED TO A STRIP ============
             🔴 NOT TO NOTHING. Collapsing the list entirely reclaims the most
             width and turns the tool off while you use it: this window's whole
             job is saying which conversation is waiting on you, and with the
             list hidden there is nowhere left for it to say so. A conversation
             could sit needing you for as long as you were reading another one.

             So the column narrows to a rail of dots instead - one per
             conversation that needs you, in its band's accent, clickable to go
             straight there. It costs about 44px of the width being reclaimed,
             which is a twentieth of what the collapse gives back.

             It shares Grid.Column 2 with ListPane and exactly one of them is
             ever visible; the column's WIDTH is what actually changes. -->
        <Border x:Name="ListStrip" Grid.Column="2" Visibility="Collapsed"
                Background="{StaticResource Panel}" CornerRadius="12" Margin="3,4,3,10"
                SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1">
          <Border.Effect>
            <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="18" ShadowDepth="3" Opacity="0.42" Color="#000000"/>
          </Border.Effect>
          <DockPanel Margin="0,11,0,11">
            <!-- The way back, on the strip itself - see the projects strip. -->
            <TextBlock x:Name="ListOpen" DockPanel.Dock="Top" Text="&#x203A;" Cursor="Hand"
                       Style="{StaticResource Caption}"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,0,9" Padding="6,2,6,2"
                       ToolTip="Show the sessions column again (Ctrl+2)"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="show the sessions column"/>
            <TextBlock x:Name="StripCount" DockPanel.Dock="Top" Style="{StaticResource Caption}"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,0,11" Text=""
                       ToolTip="Conversations waiting on you"/>
            <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Hidden" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
              <ItemsControl x:Name="StripList"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="conversations that need you">
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate>
                    <Border Background="Transparent" Cursor="Hand" Padding="0,6,0,6"
                            ToolTip="{Binding Tip}">
                      <Ellipse Width="10" Height="10" Fill="{Binding Accent}"
                               HorizontalAlignment="Center"/>
                    </Border>
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              </ItemsControl>
            </ScrollViewer>
          </DockPanel>
        </Border>

        <GridSplitter x:Name="ListSplit" Grid.Column="3" Width="4" HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                      Background="Transparent" ResizeBehavior="PreviousAndNext"/>

        <!-- the output pane: header, transcript, pinned question, composer -->
        <Border x:Name="OutputPane" Grid.Column="4" Background="{StaticResource Panel}"
                CornerRadius="12" Margin="3,4,10,10" SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1">
          <Border.Effect>
            <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="18" ShadowDepth="3" Opacity="0.42" Color="#000000"/>
          </Border.Effect>
        <Grid>
          <!-- 0 header · 1 transcript · 2 RUNNING SHELLS · 3 a question · 4 the
               composer. The shells row earns its own line rather than sharing
               the question's: two Auto elements in ONE Grid row do not stack,
               they overlap, and the two are visible at the same time exactly
               when it matters most - a session that is asking you something
               while a build runs behind it. -->
          <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
          </Grid.RowDefinitions>

          <Border Grid.Row="0" Background="{StaticResource Panel}"
                  BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="0,0,0,1" Padding="18,12,18,12">
            <!-- ================== THREE STACKED ROWS ==================
                 🔴 THE NAME AND THE BUTTONS NO LONGER SHARE A LINE. They did,
                 in two columns, and the buttons are Auto-width: six of them
                 take what they need and the name got the remainder, which on a
                 pane this wide was about a fifth of the header. So the title
                 read "millwright-str..." and the state line under it ran out
                 of room mid-word - two pieces of text ending in the same place
                 for no reason a reader could see, which is what "overlapping"
                 looked like. Stacked, each row has the whole width and nothing
                 is competing for it.
                 ======================================================== -->
            <Grid>
              <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
              </Grid.RowDefinitions>
              <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
              </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
              <StackPanel Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <TextBlock x:Name="PaneName" Style="{StaticResource H1}" Text="" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"/>
                <!-- 🪤 A GRID, NOT A HORIZONTAL StackPanel. A horizontal stack
                     measures its children against infinite width, so the state
                     line never learned it had run out of room: it was cut off
                     mid-word at the column edge and read as if it ran UNDER the
                     button strip. In a Grid the text column is star-width, so
                     it is measured against what is actually left and trims with
                     an ellipsis the way the name above it always has. -->
                <Grid Margin="0,2,0,0">
                  <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                  </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                  <Border x:Name="PaneStateDot" Grid.Column="0" Width="7" Height="7" CornerRadius="4"
                          Background="{StaticResource AccIdle}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,7,0"/>
                  <TextBlock x:Name="PaneState" Grid.Column="1" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" Text=""
                             TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"/>
                </Grid>
                <!-- ================== THE VITALS ==================
                     What the terminal's own status line shows, for the session
                     you are looking at: which model, how much context it has
                     spent, the branch, the working tree against HEAD, whether
                     Remote Control is on, and what is running right now.

                     Every figure is already on disk - `message.model` and
                     `message.usage` on an assistant record, `gitBranch` on
                     every record, a Task or background Bash whose result has
                     not come back - so none of it costs a question to claude.
                     Only the +N -N asks git, and that answer is cached.
                     ================================================ -->
              </StackPanel>
              <!-- 🪤 A WrapPanel, RIGHT-ALIGNED, ON ITS OWN ROW. Right-aligned
                   so the eye still finds the actions where it always did, and
                   wrapping so a narrow pane drops the last button to a second
                   line instead of pushing it off the edge. -->
              <WrapPanel Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" Orientation="Horizontal"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,10,0,0">
                <Button x:Name="PaneCompact" Content="Compact" Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}" Margin="0,0,6,4"
                        ToolTip="Send /compact into this conversation now - watch the context bar to the left"/>
                <Button x:Name="PaneTools" Content="Steps: folded" Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}" Margin="0,0,6,4"
                        ToolTip="How much of the machinery this pane shows - folded, full, or hidden. Remembered."/>
                <!-- Beside Steps because they are the same kind of control: a
                     view setting that belongs to the person reading, cycles on
                     click, and is remembered. It scales the WHOLE window, not
                     this pane - the list, the rail and the buttons move with
                     it - which is why it says "Text" and not "Zoom pane". -->
                <Button x:Name="PaneZoom" Content="Text: 100%" Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}" Margin="0,0,6,4"
                        ToolTip="How big every size on this surface is - 80% to 150%. Moves the list, the rail and the reading pane together. Remembered."/>
                <Button x:Name="PaneWorktree" Content="New on worktree" Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}" Margin="0,0,6,4"
                        ToolTip="Start a fresh conversation in another worktree of this project"/>
                <Button x:Name="PaneSettings" Content="Settings" Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}" Margin="0,0,6,4"
                        ToolTip="Name, model, effort, permissions, Remote Control, hidden"/>
                <!-- 🔴 IT PRESSES Esc, IT DOES NOT KILL ANYTHING. Beside
                     Relaunch on purpose: they are the two ways to stop a
                     conversation doing what it is doing, and they differ in
                     exactly the way the operator cares about - this one leaves
                     the session and its turn-so-far alive, Relaunch does not.
                     Enabled only while something is actually running; see
                     Get-InterruptBlocker, which refuses on everything else
                     because Esc at a prompt means something else entirely. -->
                <Button x:Name="PaneStop" Content="Interrupt" Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}" Margin="0,0,6,4"
                        ToolTip="Press Esc in this conversation to stop the turn it is running. Nothing is closed and nothing is lost from the transcript."/>
                <Button x:Name="PaneGoTo" Content="Go to terminal" Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}" Margin="0,0,6,4"/>
                <Button x:Name="PaneRelaunch" Content="Relaunch" Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
              </WrapPanel>
              <!-- 🪤 ITS OWN ROW, SPANNING BOTH COLUMNS. Inside the left column
                   it only ever got the width the five buttons left over, so on
                   a live session it wrapped onto two lines and the strip read
                   as a stack rather than as a status line. Across the full
                   header it is one line, which is what makes it scannable. -->
              <WrapPanel x:Name="PaneChips" Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" Margin="0,11,0,0"/>
            </Grid>
          </Border>

          <!-- ================== THE CONTROL PLANE ==================
               🔴 EVERY FIELD HERE IS A LAUNCH FLAG. claude reads them once, at
               startup, so nothing changed here reaches a session that is already
               running until it is reopened. The panel says so out loud and the
               Apply button offers the relaunch - a dropdown that silently did
               nothing would be worse than no dropdown.
               ====================================================== -->
          <!-- ================== SEND TO MANY ==================
               One line into several conversations at once. It is the thing a
               twenty-session day actually needs, and it is also the most
               dangerous button in the window - so it names every conversation
               it will type into, and it will not touch one that is mid-turn.
               ================================================== -->
          <Border x:Name="CastBox" Grid.Row="1" Visibility="Collapsed" Panel.ZIndex="20"
                  VerticalAlignment="Top" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,10,18,0"
                  Width="470" Background="{StaticResource PanelHi}" CornerRadius="10"
                  BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1" Padding="18,16,18,16">
            <Border.Effect>
              <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="26" ShadowDepth="5" Opacity="0.55" Color="#000000"/>
            </Border.Effect>
            <StackPanel>
              <TextBlock Text="SEND TO MANY" Style="{StaticResource Caption}" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
              <TextBlock x:Name="CastWho" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Margin="0,0,0,10" Text=""/>
              <ListBox x:Name="CastList" Style="{StaticResource Plain}" MaxHeight="240"
                       ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource PlainItem}" Margin="0,0,0,12">
                <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate>
                    <Border Padding="0,5,0,5" Cursor="Hand">
                      <Grid>
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <Border Grid.Column="0" x:Name="CastTick" Width="15" Height="15" CornerRadius="3"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center" BorderBrush="#3A3F47" BorderThickness="1.4"
                                Background="{Binding TickBg}" Margin="0,0,10,0"/>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Name}" Style="{StaticResource Body}"
                                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding Why}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="10,0,0,0"/>
                      </Grid>
                    </Border>
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
              </ListBox>
              <!-- 🪤 THE COMPOSER STYLE, BUT AcceptsReturn STAYS FALSE. This
                   wraps and grows so a long message can be read back before it
                   goes out - which is the whole complaint - but a literal
                   newline here would be an ENTER in every terminal it reaches,
                   submitting the message half-typed. Wrapping is visual;
                   AcceptsReturn is what a keystroke does. -->
              <TextBox x:Name="CastText" Style="{StaticResource Composer}" AcceptsReturn="False"
                       Tag="The one line every ticked conversation will receive" Margin="0,0,0,12"/>
              <Grid>
                <!-- 🔴 IT FILLS THE BOX, IT DOES NOT SEND. Twenty sessions
                     compacting is not something to set off with one press and
                     no chance to read what is going out - and the wording is
                     the whole point of the feature, so it has to be visible
                     and editable before it goes. Press this, read it, then
                     Send, exactly as with anything else typed here. -->
                <Button x:Name="CastCompact" Content="Morning compact" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                        Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}"
                        ToolTip="Fill the box with /compact plus the brief that tells each session what to keep. Nothing is sent until you press Send."/>
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
                  <Button x:Name="CastCancel" Content="Cancel" Style="{StaticResource Btn}" Margin="0,0,8,0"/>
                  <Button x:Name="CastSend" Content="Send" Style="{StaticResource BtnPrimary}"/>
                </StackPanel>
              </Grid>
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- 🪤 ROW 1, NOT ROW 0. Row 0 is Height="Auto", so a 470px panel
               placed there would stretch the HEADER to 470px rather than float
               over the transcript. Row 1 is the star row: nothing put in it can
               change the layout of anything else. -->
          <Border x:Name="SettingsBox" Grid.Row="1" Visibility="Collapsed" Panel.ZIndex="20"
                  VerticalAlignment="Top" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,10,18,0"
                  Width="430" Background="{StaticResource PanelHi}" CornerRadius="10"
                  BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1" Padding="18,16,18,16">
            <Border.Effect>
              <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="26" ShadowDepth="5" Opacity="0.55" Color="#000000"/>
            </Border.Effect>
            <StackPanel>
              <TextBlock Text="THIS CONVERSATION" Style="{StaticResource Caption}" Margin="0,0,0,12"/>

              <TextBlock Text="Name" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
              <TextBox x:Name="SetName" Style="{StaticResource Search}" Tag="What this conversation is called"
                       Margin="0,0,0,12"/>

              <Grid Margin="0,0,0,12">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="12"/>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <StackPanel Grid.Column="0">
                  <TextBlock Text="Model" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
                  <ComboBox x:Name="SetModel" Style="{StaticResource Drop}"/>
                </StackPanel>
                <StackPanel Grid.Column="2">
                  <TextBlock Text="Effort" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
                  <ComboBox x:Name="SetEffort" Style="{StaticResource Drop}"/>
                </StackPanel>
              </Grid>

              <TextBlock Text="Permission mode" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
              <ComboBox x:Name="SetPerm" Style="{StaticResource Drop}" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
              <TextBlock x:Name="SetPermNote" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Margin="0,0,0,12" Text=""/>

              <CheckBox x:Name="SetRemote" Style="{StaticResource Check}" Margin="0,0,0,8"
                        Content="Remote Control - drive this conversation from your phone"/>
              <CheckBox x:Name="SetHidden" Style="{StaticResource Check}" Margin="0,0,0,4"
                        Content="Hidden - run with no terminal window"/>
              <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Meta}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="22,0,0,12"
                         Text="It keeps running when this tool closes, and you can still read and answer it here."/>

              <!-- TOOL LIMITS. Last of the controls and deliberately plainest:
                   a deny list that is subtly wrong does not announce itself, it
                   just makes the session unable to do something, once, in an
                   hour's time. Space-separated, claude's own syntax. -->
              <Expander x:Name="SetToolsFold" Header="Tool limits" Margin="0,4,0,10"
                        Foreground="{StaticResource TextMid}" FontFamily="{DynamicResource FontText}" FontSize="{DynamicResource SzBody}">
                <StackPanel Margin="0,8,0,0">
                  <TextBlock Text="Allow only these" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
                  <!-- A real allow-list runs well past one line, and it was
                       clipped: the tools you had granted scrolled off to the
                       left where they could not be read back. Wraps and grows;
                       both are parsed on whitespace so a wrap costs nothing. -->
                  <TextBox x:Name="SetAllow" Style="{StaticResource Composer}" AcceptsReturn="False"
                           Margin="0,0,0,9" Tag="e.g.  Read Grep Bash(git *)"/>
                  <TextBlock Text="Never these" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
                  <TextBox x:Name="SetDeny" Style="{StaticResource Composer}" AcceptsReturn="False"
                           Margin="0,0,0,6" Tag="e.g.  Bash(rm *) Write"/>
                  <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Meta}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                             Text="Space separated, exactly as claude takes them. Leave both empty for no limits at all."/>
                </StackPanel>
              </Expander>

              <TextBlock x:Name="SetPending" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Foreground="{StaticResource AccNeeds}" Visibility="Collapsed" Margin="0,0,0,10" Text=""/>

              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
                <Button x:Name="SetCancel" Content="Cancel" Style="{StaticResource Btn}" Margin="0,0,8,0"/>
                <Button x:Name="SetApply" Content="Apply" Style="{StaticResource BtnPrimary}"/>
              </StackPanel>
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <!-- The transcript. A tail budget, said out loud at the top when it
               is truncated: the biggest conversation on this machine is 2.5 MB
               and a FlowDocument does not virtualize. -->
          <Grid Grid.Row="1">
            <FlowDocumentScrollViewer x:Name="PaneDoc"
                                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                                      Padding="0" Margin="0"
                                      Background="Transparent"
                                      IsSelectionEnabled="True"
                                      AutomationProperties.Name="conversation"/>
            <TextBlock x:Name="PaneEmpty" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" Text=""
                       HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="420" TextAlignment="Center"/>

            <!-- ============ WHAT THE SESSION'S OWN SCREEN SAYS, RIGHT NOW ======
                 🔴 A COMPACT WRITES NOTHING TO THE TRANSCRIPT UNTIL IT FINISHES.
                 Measured: the summary record and compactMetadata land at the END,
                 so for the thirty to ninety seconds it runs there is literally
                 nothing on disk for a transcript reader to draw. Reported as
                 "when I compact a session, the session is not shown any longer
                 or I do not see the progress of what is happening".

                 The progress exists - it is on the session's screen, where
                 claude prints its spinner and its token counts. This reads that
                 screen and shows it, and it sits in the TRANSCRIPT'S OWN CELL
                 rather than in a new row: while a compact runs the transcript
                 has nothing to add, so replacing it costs nothing and the
                 conversation comes straight back when the summary lands.

                 🪤 IT IS NOT A TERMINAL. No input goes here, nothing is
                 interpreted; it is a photograph of the last screen, taken every
                 couple of seconds while something is worth watching. NoWrap
                 because a console screen is already laid out in columns and
                 rewrapping it would scramble a spinner into prose. -->
            <Border x:Name="LivePane" Visibility="Collapsed"
                    Background="{StaticResource Ink}" CornerRadius="10"
                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource HueAskEdge}" BorderThickness="1"
                    Padding="16,13,16,14">
              <DockPanel>
                <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Top" Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,0,0,10">
                  <TextBlock x:Name="LiveMark" Text="&#x2014;" Foreground="{StaticResource HueAsk}"
                             FontFamily="{DynamicResource FontPane}" FontSize="{DynamicResource SzPane}"
                             Margin="0,0,12,0"/>
                  <TextBlock x:Name="LiveHead" Text="" Foreground="{StaticResource HueAsk}"
                             FontFamily="{DynamicResource FontPane}" FontSize="{DynamicResource SzPane}"/>
                </StackPanel>
                <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                  <TextBlock x:Name="LiveText" Text="" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                             Foreground="{StaticResource TextHigh}"
                             FontFamily="{DynamicResource FontPane}" FontSize="{DynamicResource SzPane}"
                             AutomationProperties.Name="what this session's terminal is showing now"/>
                </ScrollViewer>
              </DockPanel>
            </Border>
          </Grid>

          <!-- PINNED, not inline. A question that scrolls away on a long
               conversation is a question you miss. -->
          <!-- 🪤 A PANEL INSIDE THE CARD, not a slab across it, and NOT a bar
               down its side. This was a hard 2px white rule across the full
               width; that became a 3px white bar on the left, which the
               operator disliked for the same reason the rule was wrong - it is
               a hard, saturated edge in a window where nothing else has one,
               and it reads as damage rather than as emphasis.
               The signal survives without it: this panel is the only surface in
               the pane with its own tinted ground and its own corner radius,
               and the eyebrow above the question is the one place HueAsk
               appears. A question you miss is the failure this panel exists to
               prevent, and being the only teal thing on screen prevents it more
               reliably than an edge does. -->
          <!-- ================== WHAT IS RUNNING BEHIND THIS SESSION ==========
               🔴 THE MARK SAID SOMETHING WAS RUNNING AND NOTHING COULD SAY
               WHAT. Reported as "I cannot see a current running shell other
               than the icon indicating there is something". The square on the
               row is a COUNT off the session's status line - no id, no command;
               the pane's shell block is a Bash(background) call that happens to
               be inside the transcript tail. Two sources, so they could
               disagree, and neither is a list. This panel is the list, and it
               comes from Get-SRLiveTasks, which pairs each launch with the
               task-notification that ends it - so it names the same shells the
               mark is counting.

               🪤 PINNED, LIKE THE QUESTION, AND FOR THE SAME REASON. A shell
               you have to scroll to find is one you stop watching. It sits
               BELOW the transcript and ABOVE the question so the newest words
               and the answer box both keep their place.

               Square, not round: the mark table reads a square as machinery and
               a circle as a sub-agent, and that distinction is the one thing
               the operator can already read at a glance. -->
          <Border x:Name="ShellBox" Grid.Row="2" Visibility="Collapsed"
                  Background="{StaticResource Rail}" CornerRadius="12"
                  BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1"
                  Margin="18,0,18,10" Padding="14,10,14,11">
            <StackPanel>
              <Grid Margin="0,0,0,2">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <TextBlock x:Name="ShellHead" Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource Caption}"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource HueTool}" Text="" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                <Button x:Name="ShellFold" Grid.Column="1" Content="hide"
                        Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}" Margin="8,0,0,0"
                        ToolTip="Collapse this panel. It comes back on its own when another shell starts."/>
              </Grid>
              <ItemsControl x:Name="ShellList" Margin="0,6,0,0">
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate>
                    <Border Background="Transparent" Padding="0,5,0,6" Cursor="Hand"
                            ToolTip="{Binding ShTip}">
                      <Grid>
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding ShMark}" Foreground="{StaticResource HueTool}"
                                   FontSize="{DynamicResource SzMicro}"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="0,3,9,0"/>
                        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1">
                          <TextBlock Text="{Binding ShDesc}" Style="{StaticResource Body}"
                                     Foreground="{StaticResource TextHigh}"
                                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"/>
                          <!-- The command as it was actually issued, in the
                               machine face, because that is what it is. -->
                          <TextBlock Text="{Binding ShCmd}" FontFamily="{DynamicResource FontMono}"
                                     FontSize="{DynamicResource SzMono}"
                                     Foreground="{StaticResource TextLow}"
                                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" Margin="0,2,0,0"/>
                          <!-- The last thing it printed. This is the whole
                               point of the panel: a running shell that shows
                               no output is indistinguishable from a hung one. -->
                          <TextBlock Text="{Binding ShOut}" FontFamily="{DynamicResource FontMono}"
                                     FontSize="{DynamicResource SzMono}"
                                     Foreground="{StaticResource TextMid}"
                                     Visibility="{Binding ShOutVis}"
                                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" Margin="0,4,0,0"/>
                        </StackPanel>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding ShAge}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                                   Margin="10,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Top"/>
                      </Grid>
                    </Border>
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              </ItemsControl>
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Border x:Name="AskBox" Grid.Row="3" Visibility="Collapsed"
                  Background="{StaticResource HueAskFilm}" CornerRadius="14"
                  Margin="14,0,14,14" SnapsToDevicePixels="True"
                  BorderBrush="{StaticResource HueAskEdge}" BorderThickness="1"
                  Padding="22,18,22,18">
            <!-- A long question with a description under every option can run
                 past the bottom of the pane, and an option you cannot reach is
                 an option you cannot pick. Capped and scrollable; the cap is
                 generous enough that the common three-option question never
                 scrolls at all.

                 🔴 THE CAP IS SET IN CODE, FROM THE WINDOW HEIGHT. It was a
                 fixed 380px, which is a third of a tall window and nearly half
                 of a short one - and the card sits in its own grid row, so
                 every pixel it takes comes off the transcript above it.
                 Reported as: the question card takes up a lot of space, and we
                 still need to read the conversation while it is up. 380 stays
                 here as the value before the first layout. See Set-AskCap. -->
            <ScrollViewer x:Name="AskScroll" MaxHeight="380" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
              <StackPanel>
                <!-- ================== THE ROUND ==================
                     🔑 A BATCHED QUESTION IS A ROUND, AND THE TERMINAL DRAWS IT
                     AS A TAB BAR. Captured off a real one 2026-08-30:

                         <-  [ ] Alpha  [x] Beta  [ ] Gamma  (v) Submit  ->

                     One tab per question named by its header, an empty box while
                     unanswered and a crossed one once answered. This window used
                     to show only the question currently on screen, so a
                     three-question round looked like one question that kept
                     changing - you could not tell how many were coming, which
                     you had done, or get back to one you wanted to change.

                     The chips ARE the navigation, exactly as they are in the
                     terminal: clicking one walks the round to it with the same
                     left/right keys a person would press.
                     ================================================ -->
                <ItemsControl x:Name="AskTabs" Margin="0,0,0,12" Visibility="Collapsed">
                  <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsPanelTemplate><WrapPanel/></ItemsPanelTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                </ItemsControl>
                <TextBlock x:Name="AskHeader" Style="{StaticResource Caption}" Text="" Margin="0,0,0,7"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource HueAsk}"/>
                <TextBlock x:Name="AskText" Style="{StaticResource Body}" Text="" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           FontSize="{DynamicResource SzStrong}" FontWeight="SemiBold" Foreground="{StaticResource TextMax}"/>
                <ItemsControl x:Name="AskOptions" Margin="0,14,0,0"/>

                <!-- ================== IN YOUR OWN WORDS ==================
                     🔴 THE TERMINAL'S "Type something" ROW IS AN INLINE EDITOR,
                     and pressing ENTER on it while it is EMPTY DECLINES THE
                     WHOLE ROUND - measured twice, both times by accident. It was
                     being drawn here as an ordinary numbered option, so the
                     window was one click away from throwing a question the
                     operator was in the middle of answering.

                     It gets a real text box instead: type, then Send. Nothing is
                     committed until there is something to commit, which is the
                     one order that cannot decline the round by mistake.
                     ======================================================= -->
                <Border x:Name="AskFreeBox" Visibility="Collapsed" Margin="0,4,0,0"
                        Background="{StaticResource PanelHi}" CornerRadius="8"
                        BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1"
                        Padding="12,10,12,11">
                  <StackPanel>
                    <TextBlock x:Name="AskFreeLabel" Style="{StaticResource Caption}" Text="IN YOUR OWN WORDS"
                               Margin="0,0,0,7"/>
                    <Grid>
                      <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                      </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                      <TextBox x:Name="AskFree" Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource Composer}"
                               Tag="Answer in your own words - Shift+Enter for a new line" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                               AutomationProperties.Name="answer this question in your own words"/>
                      <Button x:Name="AskFreeSend" Grid.Column="1" Content="Send"
                              VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                              Style="{StaticResource BtnSlim}"
                              ToolTip="Type your answer into this question in the terminal and commit it"/>
                    </Grid>
                  </StackPanel>
                </Border>

                <!-- The Submit tab is a review of the whole round: every question
                     with the answer it currently holds. It is the one screen that
                     shows how everything has been answered. -->
                <ItemsControl x:Name="AskReview" Margin="0,4,0,0" Visibility="Collapsed"/>

                <!-- The note that qualifies the WHOLE question. Below the
                     buttons, because that is where it was written. -->
                <TextBlock x:Name="AskFooter" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" Text="" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Margin="0,4,0,0" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
                <TextBlock x:Name="AskNote" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Text="" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Margin="0,9,0,0" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
              </StackPanel>
            </ScrollViewer>
          </Border>

          <Border Grid.Row="4" Background="{StaticResource Panel}"
                  BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="0,1,0,0" Padding="18,10,18,12">
            <Grid>
              <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
              </Grid.RowDefinitions>
              <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
              </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

              <!-- ============ WHAT IS WAITING BEHIND THE TURN IT IS ON ========
                   🔴 DOCKED HERE, NOT INSIDE THE TRANSCRIPT. The design this
                   came from drew queued messages as ghost turns at the foot of
                   the conversation, and the pane will not have it: it appends
                   new turns to the end of the FlowDocument on a fast path
                   (Test-CanAppend / Add-ReadDocumentTail), so anything parked at
                   the bottom ends up ABOVE the replies that arrive after it.
                   Fighting that would mean stripping and re-adding the ghosts on
                   every append, to put them somewhere you can only see if you
                   are already scrolled to the end.
                   Docked, it is always visible, it costs the transcript nothing,
                   and it sits exactly where the next message is about to be
                   typed - which is the one place it is certain to be read.

                   🪤 CAPPED, because a real queue is not two lines. Measured on
                   this machine: one conversation was holding 57. Four, then a
                   count - the list is there to tell you what is waiting, not to
                   become the thing you have to scroll. -->
              <StackPanel x:Name="QueueBox" Grid.Row="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                          Visibility="Collapsed" Margin="0,0,0,9">
                <TextBlock x:Name="QueueHead" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" Text=""
                           Margin="0,0,0,5"/>
                <ItemsControl x:Name="QueueList">
                  <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate>
                      <Grid Margin="0,0,0,3">
                        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="&#187;" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                                   Foreground="{Binding QiBrush}" Margin="0,0,9,0"
                                   VerticalAlignment="Top"/>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding QiText}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                                   Foreground="{Binding QiBrush}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                   ToolTip="{Binding QiTip}"/>
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Text="{Binding QiWhen}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                                   Margin="12,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Top"/>
                      </Grid>
                    </DataTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                </ItemsControl>
              </StackPanel>

              <TextBlock x:Name="SendNote" Grid.Row="1" Grid.ColumnSpan="2" Style="{StaticResource Dim}"
                         Text="" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,6" Visibility="Collapsed"/>
              <!-- MaxHeight raised from the style's 168: this is the one you
                   paste a whole prompt into, and nine lines was not enough to
                   read one back before sending. Sixteen-ish lines, then it
                   scrolls, and the transcript above it still owns the rest. -->
              <TextBox x:Name="SendBox" Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource Composer}"
                       MaxHeight="260"
                       Tag="Type here to send it into this session's terminal - / for skills, Shift+Enter for a new line"
                       Margin="0,0,8,0"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="type into this session"/>

              <!-- THE SKILL PICKER. Opens on '/' at the start of the line, the
                   same gesture that opens it inside claude itself, and filters
                   as you type. Placement=Top so it grows UPWARDS out of the
                   composer instead of off the bottom of the window. -->
              <Popup x:Name="SkillPop" PlacementTarget="{Binding ElementName=SendBox}"
                     Placement="Top" AllowsTransparency="True" StaysOpen="True"
                     HorizontalOffset="0" VerticalOffset="-6">
                <Border Background="{StaticResource PanelHi}" CornerRadius="9"
                        BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1"
                        MinWidth="460" MaxWidth="620" Padding="0,6,0,6">
                  <Border.Effect>
                    <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="24" ShadowDepth="5" Opacity="0.55" Color="#000000"/>
                  </Border.Effect>
                  <StackPanel>
                    <TextBlock x:Name="SkillHint" Style="{StaticResource Caption}"
                               Margin="14,4,14,7" Text="SKILLS"/>
                    <ListBox x:Name="SkillList" Style="{StaticResource Plain}" MaxHeight="260"
                             ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource PlainItem}">
                      <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                        <DataTemplate>
                          <Border Padding="14,7,14,7">
                            <Grid>
                              <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
                                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
                              </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                              <StackPanel Grid.Column="0">
                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Label}" Style="{StaticResource Body}"
                                           FontWeight="SemiBold"/>
                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Blurb}" Style="{StaticResource Meta}"
                                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" Margin="0,2,0,0"/>
                              </StackPanel>
                              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Source}"
                                         Style="{StaticResource Meta}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                         Margin="12,0,0,0"/>
                            </Grid>
                          </Border>
                        </DataTemplate>
                      </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                    </ListBox>
                  </StackPanel>
                </Border>
              </Popup>
              <!-- Bottom-aligned because the box beside it grows now: stretched,
                   the button would grow with it and become a tall slab. -->
              <Button x:Name="SendBtn" Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1" Content="Send"
                      VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                      Style="{StaticResource BtnPrimary}" IsEnabled="False"/>
            </Grid>
          </Border>
        </Grid>
        </Border>
      </Grid>

      <!-- ===== SESSION MANAGER: one full-width table =====
           Deliberately a different SHAPE, not a different colour. Ported in
           phase 4; the frame exists here so the switch is real from phase 1. -->
      <!-- 🔴 ONE CARD, ON THE SAME GROUND AS THE WORK SURFACE. The manager was
           a flat full-bleed table while the work surface had become three
           floating cards, so switching surfaces switched design languages
           halfway through the window. It is deliberately a different SHAPE - one
           wide table rather than three columns - but it is the same material. -->
      <Grid x:Name="ManageSurface" Visibility="Collapsed" Background="{StaticResource Ink}">
        <Border Background="{StaticResource Panel}" CornerRadius="12" Margin="10,4,10,10"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="1"
                SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
          <Border.Effect>
            <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="18" ShadowDepth="3" Opacity="0.42" Color="#000000"/>
          </Border.Effect>
        <Grid>
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
          <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>
        <Border Grid.Row="0" Background="{StaticResource PanelHi}" CornerRadius="11,11,0,0"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="0,0,0,1" Padding="18,11,18,11">
          <!-- THE SAME COLUMN WIDTHS AS ManageRowTpl, and for the same reason a
               table has a header row at all: a caption made of padded spaces
               lines up with nothing, because the rows below it are a Grid and
               the caption was not. Change a width here, change it there. -->
          <Grid x:Name="ManageCaption">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="86"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="260"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="180"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <!-- 🔴 EACH ONE SORTS. They were inert TextBlocks that looked exactly
                 like the sortable headers of every other table on the machine,
                 which is why the operator clicked one and reported the filter
                 broken. Tag carries the sort key; the arrow says which way. -->
            <TextBlock x:Name="HdrLogon" Grid.Column="0" Tag="logon" Text="LOGON"
                       Style="{StaticResource Caption}" HorizontalAlignment="Center" Cursor="Hand"/>
            <TextBlock x:Name="HdrName" Grid.Column="1" Tag="name" Text="CONVERSATION"
                       Style="{StaticResource Caption}" Margin="14,0,10,0" Cursor="Hand"/>
            <TextBlock x:Name="HdrLane" Grid.Column="2" Tag="lane" Text="LANE"
                       Style="{StaticResource Caption}" Margin="0,0,10,0" Cursor="Hand"/>
            <TextBlock x:Name="HdrSaid" Grid.Column="3" Tag="said" Text="WHAT IT LAST SAID"
                       Style="{StaticResource Caption}" Cursor="Hand"/>
            <TextBlock x:Name="HdrAge" Grid.Column="4" Tag="age" Text="AGE"
                       Style="{StaticResource Caption}" Margin="10,0,0,0" Cursor="Hand"/>
          </Grid>
        </Border>
        <!-- 🔴 THE FILTER STRIP. The manager had sorting and nothing else - no
             way to ask "just the ticked ones" or "just what is running", which
             is the question this surface exists to answer before a logon. The
             chips are exclusive: a filter that can be half-on in four
             directions at once is a state nobody can read off the screen. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="1" Background="{StaticResource Rail}"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
                Padding="18,8,18,8">
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
            <TextBlock Text="SHOW" Style="{StaticResource Caption}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       Margin="0,0,12,0"/>
            <RadioButton x:Name="MgrAll" Content="Everything" Style="{StaticResource Seg}"
                         GroupName="mgrfilter" IsChecked="True"/>
            <RadioButton x:Name="MgrTicked" Content="Ticked" Style="{StaticResource Seg}"
                         GroupName="mgrfilter"/>
            <RadioButton x:Name="MgrRunning" Content="Running" Style="{StaticResource Seg}"
                         GroupName="mgrfilter"/>
            <RadioButton x:Name="MgrNeeds" Content="Needs you" Style="{StaticResource Seg}"
                         GroupName="mgrfilter"/>
            <TextBlock x:Name="MgrFilterNote" Style="{StaticResource Meta}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       Margin="14,0,0,0" Text=""/>
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <ListBox x:Name="ManageList" Grid.Row="2" Style="{StaticResource Plain}"
                 ItemContainerStyle="{StaticResource PlainItem}"
                 ItemTemplate="{StaticResource ManageRowTpl}"
                 AutomationProperties.Name="the ticked set"/>
        <Border Grid.Row="3" Background="{StaticResource Rail}" CornerRadius="0,0,11,11"
                BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="0,1,0,0" Padding="18,12,18,12">
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
            <TextBlock Text="THE TICKED SET" Style="{StaticResource Caption}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,14,0"/>
            <Button x:Name="OpenNotRunning" Content="Open not running" Style="{StaticResource BtnPrimary}" Margin="0,0,8,0"/>
            <Button x:Name="RelaunchSessions" Content="Relaunch sessions" Style="{StaticResource Btn}"/>
            <TextBlock x:Name="ManageCount" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="14,0,0,0"/>
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>
        </Grid>
        </Border>
      </Grid>
    </Grid>

    <!-- ========== 3. status ========== -->
    <Border Grid.Row="3" Background="{StaticResource Panel}"
            BorderBrush="{StaticResource Hairline}" BorderThickness="0,1,0,0" Padding="18,8,18,8">
      <Grid>
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="*"/>
          <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto"/>
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
        <TextBlock x:Name="Status" Grid.Column="0" Style="{StaticResource Dim}" Text=""
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"/>
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal">
          <!-- No Close here any more: the title bar has one, and two buttons
               that do the same thing in one window is one too many. The '?'
               that used to sit here had no handler at all - a control that does
               nothing when pressed is the exact defect this window was rebuilt
               to stop having. -->
          <Button x:Name="SaveBtn" Content="Save" Style="{StaticResource BtnPrimary}"/>
        </StackPanel>
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <!-- ================== 4. THE SHEET ==================
         Every question the window asks used to be a stock Windows MessageBox:
         a grey 3D dialog in Segoe UI with a shield icon, thrown up in front of
         a dark window that shares none of its vocabulary. It was also the only
         part of the tool that could not be restyled, so the more the window
         improved the more the dialogs stood out - and they appear at exactly
         the moments that matter (closing live sessions, discarding ticks).

         🪤 SCRIM AND CARD ARE TWO ELEMENTS ON PURPOSE, NOT ONE PANEL.
         The scrim has to cover the HEADER too or the dim reads as half-applied,
         which means spanning rows 0-3 - and rows 0, 1 and 3 are Height="Auto".
         Anything with a real desired size placed across them inflates them
         (the same trap already documented on SettingsBox above). A Rectangle
         with no Width/Height has a desired size of ZERO and cannot inflate
         anything, while still painting across the whole span. The card, which
         does have a size, therefore lives alone in row 2 - the star row, where
         nothing it does can change the layout of anything else.
         ================================================== -->
    <Rectangle x:Name="Scrim" Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="4" Panel.ZIndex="100"
               Visibility="Collapsed" Fill="#000000" Opacity="0.62"/>

    <Grid x:Name="Sheet" Grid.Row="2" Panel.ZIndex="101" Visibility="Collapsed">
      <Border MaxWidth="560" MaxHeight="480" VerticalAlignment="Center"
              HorizontalAlignment="Center" Margin="24"
              Background="{StaticResource Sheet}" CornerRadius="14"
              BorderBrush="{StaticResource EdgeLit}" BorderThickness="1"
              Padding="26,22,26,20" SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
        <Border.Effect>
          <DropShadowEffect BlurRadius="52" ShadowDepth="16" Opacity="0.7" Color="#000000"/>
        </Border.Effect>
        <Grid>
          <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
          </Grid.RowDefinitions>

          <!-- Display, not H1. At 15px the question read at exactly the same
               size as the pane name behind it, which is the wrong weighting for
               the only thing on screen the operator is being asked to answer. -->
          <TextBlock x:Name="SheetTitle" Grid.Row="0" Style="{StaticResource Display}"
                     TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,14" Text=""/>

          <!-- The bodies name every conversation an action will touch, so this
               scrolls rather than trimming: a list you cannot read to the end
               is not a confirmation. -->
          <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="1" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                        HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled" Margin="0,0,0,20">
            <TextBlock x:Name="SheetBody" Style="{StaticResource Body}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap" LineHeight="20" Text=""/>
          </ScrollViewer>

          <!-- Three at most, right-aligned, the primary last: the rightmost
               button is the one Enter presses and the one the eye lands on. -->
          <StackPanel Grid.Row="2" Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
            <Button x:Name="SheetB1" Style="{StaticResource Btn}" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                    Visibility="Collapsed" MinWidth="92"/>
            <Button x:Name="SheetB2" Style="{StaticResource Btn}" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                    Visibility="Collapsed" MinWidth="92"/>
            <Button x:Name="SheetB3" Style="{StaticResource BtnPrimary}"
                    Visibility="Collapsed" MinWidth="92"/>
          </StackPanel>
        </Grid>
      </Border>
    </Grid>
  </Grid>
  </Border>
</Window>
